+# Design audit follow-up, 2026-10-16
+
+## Scope and revision
+
+Static audit of `0d3fbc1a1821074d6ba8a2a212116241adc85c4e`, after
+#923/#924/#925 were merged through #927/#928/#926.
+The user requested software-design-philosophy, particular attention to types,
+Enums and obsolete compatibility work, and authorized filing justified Issues.
+Compatibility preservation is not required.
+
+Worktree: `.worktrees/design-audit-20261016`.
+Branch: `audit/2026-10-16-design-followup`, created from fetched origin/main;
+upstream removed to avoid accidentally pushing to main.
+Origin fetch/push URL: `https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin.git`.
+
+Read the knowledge catalog and evidence guide, inspected code and existing
+tests, and checked open and recently closed GitHub Issues. Parallel read-only
+reviews covered runtime/workspace/session, analyzer/plan/review, and
+configuration/report/Python compatibility. Parent review additionally examined
+progress, metrics and report delivery, and independently checked every accepted
+finding. No production source or test was changed.
+
+## Accepted improvements
+
+| Issue | Evidence and smallest useful change |
+| --- | --- |
+| [#929](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/929) | Remove three producerless analyzer diagnostic variants and their mappings. #851 preserved shared definitions verbatim; #865 centralized the retained names. Keep generic message/location coverage using current variants. |
+| [#930](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/930) | Return existing MutationOperator values from token replacement selection. Remove the immediate string-to-enum parse and expect; preserve candidate string serialization and ordering. |
+| [#931](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/931) | ApplyMutation already owns the candidate, but the synchronous handler takes a second candidate and ignores the request payload. Remove the extra argument; keep synchronous and owned execution APIs. #872 explicitly preserved this difference. |
+
+These are maintenance improvements, not reproduced failures of the current CLI.
+The workspace signature change is deliberate; no old-signature wrapper is needed.
+The diagnostic deletion does not require rejecting arbitrary saved diagnostic
+strings. Token selection should not change all candidate operator fields to an
+Enum: declaration ordering differs from current lexical ordering.
+
+## Candidates not promoted to Issues
+
+- Saved normalized arrays and session budgets: the recent changes already reject
+  obsolete missing fields and old schemas. Raw configuration defaults still
+  serve current input.
+- Git legacy diff and Windows deletion fallback: current rename/unmerged inputs
+  and filesystem behavior require these paths; names alone do not establish
+  obsolete compatibility.
+- SelectionWire and flat disk reporting: projections describe current external
+  metadata. Internal enums already centralize their relationships; a schema
+  redesign has no established net benefit here.
+- CandidateValidationContext Result/Option fields: private construction and
+  validation precedence enforce their relationship; a new wrapper would not
+  simplify the sole consumer.
+- Review SourceState versus Applicability: source observations and stored-key
+  matching are separate responsibilities.
+- All candidate operator strings becoming Enums: this would broaden persistence,
+  stable-ID and ordering changes beyond the confirmed internal conversion.
+- Report optional fields, progress opaque configuration and Python tool optional
+  evidence: current consumers legitimately omit or do not require these values.
+- Release Result-to-exception adapters and target effect handlers: they implement
+  current error/ID boundaries rather than preserving retired transport.
+- Progress pair versus aggregate state and metrics stage strings: inspected
+  their construction/consumption; localized mappings do not establish enough
+  present complexity reduction to justify another standalone Issue.
+- Removing every synchronous workspace API: useful recovery and Lean adapter
+  tests consume them; the confirmed problem only requires one argument removal.
+- CLI error classification and language splitting: no separate proposal with
+  demonstrated benefit beyond existing #743.
+
+## Philosophy assessment
+
+Scoped qualitative score: **7.5/10**, six diagnostic rows supported, one
+partially unmet and one unmeasured. This is not a complete correctness result,
+nor a numerical comparison with earlier audits of different boundaries.
+
+| Diagnostic | Assessment |
+| --- | --- |
+| Module purpose can be stated in one sentence | Supported: pure core policy, analysis, filesystem workspace, reporting, persistence have identifiable ownership. |
+| Interface simpler than implementation | Supported overall: owned tasks, verified plans and validated configuration hide substantial work. |
+| Implementation changes remain local | Partially unmet: synchronous callers duplicate candidate data/selection knowledge; fixed operator IDs are converted through a string agreement. #929 also reduces obsolete vocabulary callers must understand. |
+| Interface comments explain abstraction | Supported by workspace ownership, artifact-reader and selection contracts; update the changed signature's contract during implementation. |
+| Design discussion is routine in reviews | Supported by recent design Issues, implementation records and explicit scope decisions in #851/#865/#872. |
+| Modules own important decisions | Supported: core owns candidate identity/configuration; workspace operations own recovery; artifact reader owns decoding. |
+| Boundaries understandable without implementation reading | Supported overall by module contracts and the knowledge catalog; remaining local vocabulary is addressed by #929. |
+| 10–20% of time invested in design | Unmeasured; do not infer time allocation from document count. |
+
+To reach the framework's 10/10 within this scope, remove the confirmed local
+duplication and obsolete vocabulary, re-review the resulting boundaries, and
+measure actual design-investment time. Time measurement is not a reason to file
+a code-change Issue.
+
+## Review passes
+
+### Scope: five passes
+
+1. Checked main SHA, fetched origin, verified URLs and isolated the audit branch.
+2. Checked #923/#924/#925 completion and excluded them from new proposals.
+3. Checked open Issues (#806/#743/#570) and recent closed titles for overlap.
+4. Read catalog/evidence policy; separated current contracts from historical results.
+5. Split independent review domains and required producer/consumer evidence;
+   rejected a quota-based search for Issues.
+
+### Evidence: five passes
+
+1. Traced real diagnostic constructors and enum consumers; obsolete values occur
+   only in mappings and synthetic construction tests.
+2. Traced token replacement into its sole conversion and typed candidate builder;
+   inspected compound tokens and existing enum canonical names.
+3. Traced request/explicit candidate behavior and the test that deliberately
+   preserves their difference; confirmed production owned tasks and cfg(test)
+   direct shell execution.
+4. Read #851/#865/#872 bodies and local maintenance provenance to distinguish
+   new follow-up scope from already completed work.
+5. Checked proposed alternatives against ordering, persisted strings, ownership,
+   recovery, and existing test/oracle callers; reduced workspace scope to one
+   source of candidate data.
+
+### Publication: five passes
+
+1. Wrote separate issue drafts with current SHA links, actual evidence,
+   acceptance criteria and explicit static-only verification scope.
+2. Checked that no proposal introduces a compatibility wrapper or speculative
+   abstraction; kept supported platform fallbacks and useful wire projections.
+3. Independently reviewed draft evidence. Corrected the token draft's claim that
+   all named tests are parameterized; added the actual annotation-suppression
+   test rather than relying on lookup-statistics coverage.
+4. Validated all fourteen source-link file/line ranges locally and checked the
+   named token regression tests exist. Re-read past Issue bodies for scope.
+5. Re-read the three published Issues (#929/#930/#931); confirmed OPEN state,
+   correct repository and titles. Checked source anchors and local change scope.
+   The source record is catalogued below without claiming runtime validation.
+
+## Verification limits
+
+This audit did not run Rust builds, tests, proptest, mutation testing, benchmarks
+or Lean proofs. Existing tests were read to define implementation acceptance
+criteria; their presence is not a new execution result. The proposed changes do
+not introduce a new state machine requiring a fresh formal model. Implementation
+should run relevant existing regression/property/oracle tests and add focused
+coverage only where needed, with AAA and one viewpoint per test.
+
+Implementation is not part of this audit. Future work
+should use separate branches with corresponding documentation and at least five
+self-review passes in each design, planning, implementation and verification stage.
+
+
+## Documentation checks
+
+The new concept YAML, source hashes, footnotes and local links passed focused
+checks. All fourteen source-code link ranges in the Issue drafts exist at the
+pinned revision. Published Issues were read back and confirmed open.
+The strict documentation-site command could not start because the existing
+Python environment lacks the markdown package; no successful site build is
+claimed. This audit only changes internal knowledge and review documents.
